Task 1: Configure Password Aging Policies
Password aging policies help ensure users regularly update their passwords, reducing security risks from compromised credentials.

Subtask 1.1: Review Current Password Policy
First, let's examine the existing password policy configuration.

Connect to your IdM server using the provided credentials in your lab environment.

Authenticate as the IdM administrator:

kinit admin
Enter the admin password when prompted (provided in your lab environment).

View the current global password policy:
ipa pwpolicy-show
Examine the output to understand current settings:
Maximum password lifetime: How long passwords remain valid
Minimum password lifetime: How soon users can change passwords
Password history size: How many previous passwords are remembered
Failure count interval: Time window for counting failed attempts
Subtask 1.2: Configure Password Aging Parameters
Now we'll modify the password policy to enforce aging requirements.

Set maximum password lifetime to 90 days:
ipa pwpolicy-mod --maxlife=90
Set minimum password lifetime to 1 day (prevents immediate password changes):
ipa pwpolicy-mod --minlife=1
Configure password history to remember last 5 passwords:
ipa pwpolicy-mod --history=5
Set password expiration warning to 7 days:
ipa pwpolicy-mod --priority=7
Verify the updated policy:
ipa pwpolicy-show
Subtask 1.3: Create Group-Specific Password Policy
Different user groups may require different password policies based on their security requirements.

Create a test group for high-security users:
ipa group-add --desc="High Security Users" highsec-users
Create a specific password policy for this group:
ipa pwpolicy-add highsec-users --maxlife=30 --minlife=2 --history=10 --minclasses=4 --minlength=12
Verify the group-specific policy:
ipa pwpolicy-show highsec-users
Task 2: Enforce Password Complexity Rules
Password complexity rules ensure users create strong passwords that resist common attack methods.

Subtask 2.1: Configure Basic Complexity Requirements
Set minimum password length to 8 characters:
ipa pwpolicy-mod --minlength=8
Require at least 3 character classes (uppercase, lowercase, numbers, special characters):
ipa pwpolicy-mod --minclasses=3
Set maximum number of consecutive identical characters to 2:
ipa pwpolicy-mod --maxrepeat=2
Configure maximum sequence length to 3 (prevents "123" or "abc" sequences):
ipa pwpolicy-mod --maxsequence=3
Subtask 2.2: Implement Advanced Complexity Rules
Enable dictionary check to prevent common words:
ipa pwpolicy-mod --dictcheck=TRUE
Configure user attribute checking to prevent passwords containing username:
ipa pwpolicy-mod --usercheck=TRUE
Set account lockout parameters:
ipa pwpolicy-mod --failurecountinterval=600 --lockouttime=1800 --maxfail=5
This configuration means:

failurecountinterval=600: Count failures within 10 minutes
lockouttime=1800: Lock account for 30 minutes after max failures
maxfail=5: Lock account after 5 failed attempts
View the complete updated policy:
ipa pwpolicy-show
Subtask 2.3: Configure Password Expiration Notifications
Set up email notifications for password expiration (if mail server is configured):
ipa config-mod --emaildomain=lab.example.com
Configure LDAP password policy attributes:
ipa pwpolicy-mod --gracelimit=3
This allows 3 grace logins after password expiration.

Task 3: Test Password Policy Enforcement
Testing ensures your password policies work as expected and don't create usability issues.

Subtask 3.1: Create Test Users
Create a regular test user:
ipa user-add testuser1 --first=Test --last=User1 --password
Enter a temporary password when prompted.

Create a high-security test user:
ipa user-add testuser2 --first=Test --last=User2 --password
Add testuser2 to the high-security group:
ipa group-add-member highsec-users --users=testuser2
Subtask 3.2: Test Password Complexity Enforcement
Test password length requirements:
echo "short" | ipa passwd testuser1
This should fail due to minimum length requirement.

Test character class requirements:
echo "password" | ipa passwd testuser1
This should fail due to insufficient character classes.

Test a compliant password:
echo "SecurePass123!" | ipa passwd testuser1
This should succeed.

Test high-security user password requirements:
echo "ComplexPass123!" | ipa passwd testuser2
This should fail due to stricter requirements for the high-security group.

Test a compliant high-security password:
echo "VeryComplexPass123!@#" | ipa passwd testuser2
This should succeed.

Subtask 3.3: Test Password History and Aging
Verify password history enforcement:
# Try to reuse the same password immediately
echo "SecurePass123!" | ipa passwd testuser1
This should fail due to password history.

Check password expiration information:
ipa user-show testuser1 --all | grep -i password
Test account lockout policy:
# Simulate failed login attempts (run this 6 times)
for i in {1..6}; do
    echo "wrongpassword" | kinit testuser1 2>/dev/null
    echo "Attempt $i completed"
done
Verify account lockout status:
ipa user-show testuser1 | grep -i lock
Unlock the test account:
ipa user-unlock testuser1
Subtask 3.4: Test Password Aging Notifications
Manually set password expiration for testing:
# Set password to expire in 2 days
ipa user-mod testuser1 --password-expiration="$(date -d '+2 days' '+%Y%m%d%H%M%SZ')"
Check expiration warning:
kinit testuser1
The system should display a warning about upcoming password expiration.

View detailed password policy information for user:
ipa pwpolicy-show --user=testuser1
Troubleshooting Common Issues
Issue 1: Password Policy Not Taking Effect
Problem: New password policies don't seem to apply to existing users.

Solution:

# Clear the credential cache and re-authenticate
kdestroy
kinit admin
# Verify policy is correctly configured
ipa pwpolicy-show
Issue 2: Users Cannot Change Passwords
Problem: Users receive errors when trying to change passwords.

Solution:

# Check if minimum password lifetime is preventing changes
ipa pwpolicy-show | grep "Min lifetime"
# If needed, temporarily reduce minimum lifetime
ipa pwpolicy-mod --minlife=0
Issue 3: Account Lockout Issues
Problem: Accounts are getting locked too frequently.

Solution:

# Adjust lockout parameters
ipa pwpolicy-mod --maxfail=10 --failurecountinterval=1800
# Unlock specific users
ipa user-unlock username
Issue 4: Group Policy Not Applied
Problem: Group-specific password policies aren't working.

Solution:

# Verify group membership
ipa group-show groupname
# Check if group policy exists
ipa pwpolicy-show groupname
# Ensure user is properly added to group
ipa group-add-member groupname --users=username
Verification Commands
Use these commands to verify your configuration:

# Check all password policies
ipa pwpolicy-find

# View specific user's effective policy
ipa pwpolicy-show --user=username

# Check user account status
ipa user-show username --all | grep -E "(password|lock|expire)"

# Test password strength
echo "testpassword" | ipa passwd username

# View policy inheritance
ipa user-show username | grep "Member of groups"
Security Best Practices
Regular Policy Review: Periodically review and update password policies based on security requirements and user feedback.

Balanced Security: Ensure policies are secure but not so restrictive that they impact productivity.

User Education: Train users on creating strong passwords that comply with policies.

Monitoring: Regularly monitor failed authentication attempts and account lockouts.

Exception Handling: Have procedures for handling legitimate lockouts and password reset requests.

